Aim:- CPU Scheduling Algorithms – Simulate FCFS, SJF, Round Robin, and Priority scheduling in C/Python.

Theory:-
What is CPU Scheduling?
CPU scheduling is the process of selecting a process from the ready queue and giving it the CPU for execution.
When several processes are ready to execute, the operating system uses a CPU scheduling algorithm to decide which process should run first.

•	Process:- A process is a program that is currently running.  Example: P1, P2, P3
•	Burst Time (BT):- The amount of CPU time required by a process.  example: P1 = 5 ms
•	Waiting Time (WT):- The amount of time a process waits in the ready queue before getting the CPU.  Example: WT = Completion Time - Burst Time 
•	Turnaround Time (TAT): Total time from when a process arrives until it finishes.  Example: TAT = Waiting Time + Burst Time
•	Time Quantum: A fixed amount of CPU time given to each process in Round Robin scheduling.  Example: Time Quantum = 2 ms






1.  FCFS Scheduling:- FCFS stands for First Come First Serve.
The process that arrives first gets the CPU first.
It works like a queue: First person in → First person served

Code:-

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int n, i;
    int bt[20], wt[20], tat[20]; //Three arrays are created.

    printf("Enter number of processes: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    printf("Enter burst time for each process:\n");
 for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("P%d: ", i + 1);
        scanf("%d", &bt[i]);
    }

    // Waiting time of first process is 0
    wt[0] = 0;

    // Calculate waiting time
    for(i = 1; i < n; i++)
    {
        wt[i] = wt[i - 1] + bt[i - 1];
    }

    // Calculate turnaround time
    for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        tat[i] = wt[i] + bt[i];
    }

    printf("\nProcess\tBT\tWT\tTAT\n");

    for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        printf("P%d\t%d\t%d\t%d\n",
               i + 1, bt[i], wt[i], tat[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}

